1) => slope = (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, which is slope -1, not sunny. So L4 fails. Could try L4: (1,4) and (2,3) but (2,3) already used. Could try (1,4) and (3,2) but (3,2) used. Could try (1,4) and (4,1) is slope -1, not allowed. Could try (1,4) and (2,2) slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. (2,2) is used by L1. Could try (1,4) and (3,1) slope = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2, sunny. (3,1) is in column 3. So L4: (1,4) and (3,1) slope = -3/2, sunny. So we have lines:\n\n- L1: (1,1)-(2,2) slope 1\n- L2: (1,2)-(2,3) slope 1\n- L3: (1,3)-(3,2) slope -1/2\n- L4: (1,4)-(3,1) slope -3/2\n\nNow check coverage: Points covered:\n\n- L1 covers (1,1),(2,2)\n- L2 covers (1,2),(2,3)\n- L3 covers (1,3),(3,2)\n- L4 covers (1,4),(3,1)\n\nRemaining points: (2,1),(2,2) already covered, (2,1) not covered yet. (2,1) is in column 2 row 1. (2,1) is not covered by any line above. (3,1) covered by L4. (4,1) not covered. (2,2) covered by L1. (2,3) covered by L2. (3,2) covered by L3. (1,4) covered by L4. (1,3) covered by L3. (1,2) covered by L2. (1,1) covered by L1. So uncovered points: (2,1) and (4,1). Also (3,? maybe (3,? ) we have (3,2) covered, (3,1) covered, (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) Actually (3,? ) points: (3,1) covered, (3,2) covered, (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) Actually column 3 has points (3,1),(3,2). So both covered. Column 4 has point (4,1) uncovered. Column 2 has points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). (2,2) covered, (2,3) covered, (2,1) uncovered. So we have two uncovered points. We have no more lines to cover them. So this construction fails.\n\nWe could try different assignment. Perhaps we can cover all points with 4 sunny lines. Let's try to systematically think: We need to cover 10 points with 4 lines, each line can cover at most one point per column, so each line can cover at most 4 points (since there are 4 columns). So total coverage capacity is at most 4*4 = 16 points, which is enough. But we need to ensure each point is covered exactly once (or at least once). The challenge is to assign points to lines such that each line passes through at most one point per column, and each line is not vertical/horizontal/diag.\n\nWe can think of this as a problem of constructing a set of 4 lines that intersect each column at exactly one of the points in that column (maybe some columns have no intersection for some lines). Since we have 4 columns, each line can intersect each column at most once. So we can think of each line as a function mapping column index a to a row b (if the line passes through a point in that column). For each line, we can define a set of columns where it passes through a point. The line is determined by two points (since it's not vertical). So each line is determined by its intersection with two columns. So we need to choose for each line two columns and assign points in those columns such that the line passes through them and is not of forbidden slopes.\n\nWe need to cover all points. Let's try to find a systematic approach: Since we have 4 lines, each line can cover at most 4 points, but we need to cover 10 points, so average 2.5 points per line. So some lines will cover 2 points, some 3 points. Let's try to assign each line to cover 3 points, except one line covering 1 point. But each line can intersect at most 4 columns, so covering 3 points means it intersects 3 columns. So we need to assign points such that each line passes through points in distinct columns.\n\nWe can try to use lines that pass through points in columns 1,2,3 (or 4). Let's try to find a line that passes through (1,1),(2,2),(3,3)? But (3,3) is not in region because a+b=6 >5. So not allowed. So we cannot have (3,3). So maybe line through (1,1),(2,2) is slope 1, passes through (3,3) which is not in region, but that's okay; the line can pass through points outside region, but we only care about covering points inside region. So line L1: slope 1 through (1,1) and (2,2). It also passes through (3,3) (outside region) and (4,4) (outside). So L1 covers (1,1),(2,2). Good.\n\nLine L2: slope 1 through (1,2) and (2,3). It also passes through (3,4) (outside region). So L2 covers (1,2),(2,3).\n\nLine L3: slope 1 through (1,3) and (2,4) (but (2,4) is not allowed because a+b=6 >5). So L3 would only cover (1,3). That's not enough.\n\nAlternatively, we can use different slopes.\n\nLet's try to use lines with slope 2: L3: through (1,3) and (2,5) (outside). So only (1,3). Not good.\n\nMaybe we need to use lines that intersect column 4 as well. Column 4 has only point (4,1). So we need at least one line that passes through (4,1). That line can also pass through points in other columns. For example, line through (4,1) and (1,4) has slope (4-1)/(1-4) = 3/(-3) = -1, not sunny. So cannot use that. Line through (4,1) and (1,3) slope = (3-1)/(1-4) = 2/(-3) = -2/3, sunny. So L4: passes through (4,1) and (1,3). It also passes through (2,? )? Let's compute line equation: slope m = -2/3, passes through (1,3): y - 3 = -2/3 (x - 1) => y = 3 - 2/3 (x - 1) = 3 - 2/3 x + 2/3 = (3 + 2/3) - (2/3)x = (11/3) - (2/3)x. For x=2, y = 11/3 - 4/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333, not integer. So no point in column 2. For x=3, y = 11/3 - 6/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.667, not integer. So L4 only covers (1,3) and (4,1). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover remaining points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2). We have L1 covering (1,1),(2,2). L2 covering (1,2),(2,3). L4 covering (1,3),(4,1). So uncovered: (1,4),(2,1),(3,1),(3,2). Also (2,? ) maybe (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,?