at most one point per column. So each column's points must be assigned to distinct lines. So for column 2, we need to assign its 4 points to the 4 lines, one each. For column 3, we need to assign its 3 points to 3 of the lines (one line will not have a point in column 3). For column 4, assign its 2 points to 2 lines. For column 5, assign its point to 1 line.\n\nThus each line will have a pattern of columns it appears in. For example, one line may appear in columns 2,3,4,5 (covering one point in each). Another line may appear in columns 2,3,4 (but not 5). Another line may appear in columns 2,3 (but not 4,5). Another line may appear only in column 2. This is a possible distribution.\n\nNow we need to find lines that satisfy the assigned points.\n\nLet's denote the lines L1, L2, L3, L4. We'll assign column 2 points as follows:\n\n- L1: (2,1)\n- L2: (2,2)\n- L3: (2,3)\n- L4: (2,4)\n\nNow we need to assign column 3 points (3,1),(3,2),(3,3) to three of the lines. Let's try to assign:\n\n- L1: (3,2) maybe? Or (3,1)? Let's try to assign (3,2) to L1 (since L1 already has (2,1)). Then slope m1 = (2 - 1) / (3 - 2) = 1. So m1 = 1. Then line L1: b = a + c1. Since (2,1) satisfies: 1 = 2 + c1 => c1 = -1. So L1: b = a - 1. Check (3,2): 2 = 3 - 1 = 2, ok. So L1 covers (2,1) and (3,2). Good. For column 4, L1 would have b = 4 - 1 = 3, but column 4 only has b ∈ {1,2}. So L1 cannot cover a point in column 4 (since b=3 not allowed). So L1 will not appear in column 4. For column 5, L1 would have b = 5 - 1 = 4, but column 5 only has b=1. So L1 cannot appear in column 5. So L1 will only cover columns 2 and 3.\n\nNow assign column 3 point (3,1) to L2 (which has (2,2)). Compute slope: m2 = (1 - 2) / (3 - 2) = -1, which is not allowed (slope -1). So cannot assign (3,1) to L2. Assign (3,3) to L2: slope = (3 - 2) / (3 - 2) = 1, allowed. So L2: passes through (2,2) and (3,3). Then m2 = 1, c2 = b - a = 2 - 2 = 0? Actually b = a + c2 => 2 = 2 + c2 => c2 = 0. So L2: b = a. Check (3,3): 3 = 3 + 0, ok